+
+> Author working draft, refined 27 September 2026 from the supplied 26 September revision. Figure 6 and Table 4 retain pre-correction contact results; corrected coupled integration remains pending.
 
 ## Abstract
 
-Landing on a floating platform depends on the motion at the intended contact points, which can differ substantially from the motion at the platform reference point. We formulate a partitioned calculation that links frequency-domain hydrodynamics, a radiation-memory platform model and a four-foot multibody landing model. Complex deck-point response operators preserve the phase relationships between translation and rotation. Individual feet can contact, lift off and re-contact, and their resultant force and moment are returned to the platform model. The evidence is separated into reproducibility checks, limited submodel comparisons and an unqualified full-scale example. In a reduced comparison with the drop tests of Yang et al., calibration on simultaneous touchdown gives buffer-stroke peak errors below 4.1% in two withheld sequences. Acceleration and force histories are not reproduced adequately, and 127 of 128 multi-start fits are near-optimal, precluding unique parameter identification. For a prescribed 120 m by 50 m barge, the sampled heave response reaches 1.113 m/m. The selected medium-to-fine mesh change is 5.88%, exceeding the 5% criterion. Random-wave screening uses 1000 realizations per condition and bootstrap intervals; 41 of 63 conditions pass a preliminary linear-motion screen. The archived landing calculation satisfies a 2% fixed-point update criterion after four passes, but local contact metrics fail refinement checks. Further implementation review identifies an incorrect transfer of the literature inertia between coordinate systems, a deck-reference mismatch and point interpolation where conservative load transfer was stated. Those landing histories are retained as pre-correction diagnostics, not corrected results. The reproducible subcalculations support the deck-motion analysis, while the full coupled landing requires corrected integration and renewed convergence tests before its loads or touchdown sequence can be interpreted physically.
+Landing on a floating platform depends on motion at the contact points, where platform rotation contributes to both displacement and velocity. A partitioned formulation combines frequency-domain wave-body interaction, finite-band radiation memory and independent four-foot contact. Complex response operators transfer platform motion to the landing footprint, and the contact wrench supplies the platform feedback. A reduced comparison with the drop tests of Yang et al. gives buffer-stroke peak errors below 4.1% for two asymmetric sequences using parameters fitted only to simultaneous touchdown. Acceleration and force histories remain discrepant, while 127 of 128 multi-start fits are near-optimal, indicating poor practical identifiability. For a prescribed 120 m by 50 m barge, the sampled heave response reaches 1.113 m/m. The selected medium-to-fine mesh change is 5.88%, above the 5% criterion. Screening with 1000 random-wave realizations per condition and bootstrap intervals places 41 of 63 conditions within a preliminary linear-motion envelope. These statistics describe finite-record deck-motion extremes, not landing success. The archived full-scale calculation meets a 2% fixed-point update criterion after four passes but fails contact-refinement checks. Its inertia mapping, collision-deck reference and executed load transfer also require correction. The corresponding landing histories are retained solely as pre-correction diagnostics. Independent subproblem reruns support computational reproducibility within the stated scope; corrected coupled integration and renewed convergence tests are required before interpreting the full-scale contact loads or touchdown sequence physically.
 
 Keywords: reusable launch vehicle; floating recovery platform; radiation memory; local deck kinematics; irregular waves; four-leg contact; partitioned coupling
 
